task: growing-onbehalf
agent: opus5-bench
started: 2026-09-08
branch: feature/growing-onbehalf
worktree: ../okay-wt-onbehalf

why: operator instruction 2026-09-08 — take the fix. `Growing` grows
  with ONE producer in 10 runs of 30 (BACKLOG growing-onep) because it
  reads `Thread.currentThread()` at push time, while
  `SentinelChannel.attemptSend` resumes a parked sender by running its
  continuation on the WAKER's thread — the consumer's. One producer,
  two apparent identities.

what: fix shape (a), the one the operator picked. The channel already
  knows: `attemptSend` carries `granted0` and captures `route` at
  entry. What is missing is a way to tell the buffer, so:
  - `Buffer.pushDecidingAtOnBehalf` — a push the channel makes ON
    BEHALF of a parked producer, DEFAULT delegating to
    `pushDecidingAt`, so no other buffer changes at all;
  - `Growing` overrides it to push WITHOUT sampling and, on refusal,
    without growing: a resumed push says nothing about who is
    producing, so it must not be evidence either way;
  - `SentinelChannel.attemptSend` calls it when `granted`.

  And the law, which today is stated at the wrong LAYER: TestGrowing's
  two "one producer never grows it" tests drive the Buffer directly
  from one thread, so they cannot see the channel's resume path. The
  new law goes through a CHANNEL with a concurrent consumer.

predict: at one producer `growing_chunk` moves from 192 toward the
  ring's ~126. DISQUALIFYING: if it lands nearer 192 than 126 the
  thread identity was not the whole cost, the rest is the wrapper's
  own dispatch, and I say so rather than credit the fix. The 4- and
  16-producer numbers (601.6 / 623.6 against adaptive's 162.1 / 116.6)
  are NOT addressed here and must not move.
